# Heartbeats and Schedules

## Overview

An Agent runs a Turn only when a Signal arrives. For timed work, such as
polling every 5 seconds, a report at the top of each hour, or a reminder in
10 minutes, two built-in Plugins produce those Signals for you:

- `Jido.Plugin.Heartbeat` sends one fixed Signal on a fixed interval.
- `Jido.Plugin.Scheduler` turns Directives returned by Actions into one-shot
  and recurring Signals.

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# A Signal every second, configured once:
plugin(Jido.Plugin.Heartbeat, config: %{interval: 1_000, signal_type: "monitor.beat"})

# A Signal decided at runtime, returned from an Action:
{:ok, state, [Jido.Plugin.Scheduler.cron("report", "0 * * * *", tick)]}
```

A timer firing is an ordinary Signal. It runs a normal Turn through your
routes.

Run the cells from top to bottom. Some cells wait up to 5 seconds for real
timers.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## Wait Helper

This helper polls a condition every 20 ms until it returns `{:ok, value}` or
the timeout passes.

```elixir
defmodule ScheduleGuide.Wait do
  def until(fun, timeout \\ 3_000) do
    deadline = System.monotonic_time(:millisecond) + timeout
    loop(fun, deadline)
  end

  defp loop(fun, deadline) do
    case fun.() do
      {:ok, value} ->
        {:ok, value}

      _not_yet ->
        if System.monotonic_time(:millisecond) >= deadline do
          :timeout
        else
          Process.sleep(20)
          loop(fun, deadline)
        end
    end
  end
end
```

## Add A Heartbeat

The Heartbeat Plugin runs a process that casts a Signal to its Agent every
`interval` ms. Route the Signal type like any other.

```elixir
defmodule ScheduleGuide.Monitor do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "monitor"

  state do
    schema(Zoi.object(%{beats: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)}))
  end

  plugins do
    plugin(Jido.Plugin.Heartbeat, config: %{interval: 100, signal_type: "monitor.beat"})
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/heartbeats")

    route "monitor.beat" do
      action _input, schema: Zoi.object(%{}), output_schema: Zoi.map(), context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | beats: context.agent_state.beats + 1}}
      end
      state from: output()
    end
  end
end
```

Start it and wait for three beats:

```elixir
{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()
{:ok, monitor} = Jido.start_agent(ScheduleGuide.Monitor, id: "monitor")

ScheduleGuide.Wait.until(fn ->
  beats = Jido.AgentServer.agent(monitor).state.beats
  if beats >= 3, do: {:ok, :beating}, else: :wait
end)
```

The result is `{:ok, :beating}`. Each beat ran a full Turn and committed.

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent("monitor")
```

The heartbeat process stops with the Agent.

### Heartbeat Options

| Option | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `interval` | `5_000` | Milliseconds between Signals. Positive integer. |
| `signal_type` | `"jido.agent.heartbeat"` | Type of each Signal. |
| `signal_data` | `%{}` | Data for each Signal. A plain map. |
| `source` | `"/plugin/heartbeat"` | Source of each Signal. |

Add a route for the heartbeat type. Without one, every tick fails its Turn
and logs an error.

Ticks are casts. While a Turn runs, ticks wait in the Agent's queue. If a
Turn takes longer than `interval`, ticks pile up until the queue reaches
`max_postponed_signals` (default 1,000) and the Server drops the rest. Set
`interval` longer than your slowest handler.

## Add The Scheduler

A heartbeat is fixed when the Agent starts. With the Scheduler, Actions
decide what to schedule while the Agent runs. Three Directives cover it:
`schedule/2`, `cron/4`, and `cancel/1`.

```elixir
defmodule ScheduleGuide.Reminders do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "reminders"

  state do
    schema(
      Zoi.object(%{
        reminders: Zoi.list(Zoi.string()) |> Zoi.default([]),
        reports: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0),
        last_occurrence: Zoi.string() |> Zoi.nullable() |> Zoi.default(nil)
      })
    )
  end

  plugins do
    plugin(Jido.Plugin.Scheduler)
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/schedules")

    route "reminder.request", as: :request do
      action %{text: text, delay_ms: delay},
        schema: Zoi.object(%{text: Zoi.string(), delay_ms: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.min(0)}),
        output_schema: Zoi.map(), context: context do
        due = Jido.Signal.new!("reminder.due", %{text: text}, source: "/guides/schedules")
        {:ok, context.agent_state, [Jido.Plugin.Scheduler.schedule(delay, due)]}
      end
      state from: output()
    end

    route "reminder.due" do
      action %{text: text}, schema: Zoi.object(%{text: Zoi.string()}), output_schema: Zoi.map(), context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | reminders: context.agent_state.reminders ++ [text]}}
      end
      state from: output()
    end

    route "report.start", as: :start_reports do
      action _input, schema: Zoi.object(%{}), output_schema: Zoi.map(), context: context do
        tick = Jido.Signal.new!("report.tick", %{}, source: "/guides/schedules")

        {:ok, context.agent_state,
         [Jido.Plugin.Scheduler.cron("report", "* * * * * * *", tick, generation: 1)]}
      end
      state from: output()
    end

    route "report.stop", as: :stop_reports do
      action _input, schema: Zoi.object(%{}), output_schema: Zoi.map(), context: context do
        {:ok, context.agent_state, [Jido.Plugin.Scheduler.cancel("report")]}
      end
      state from: output()
    end

    route "report.tick" do
      action _input, schema: Zoi.object(%{}), output_schema: Zoi.map(), context: context do
        {:ok, occurrence} = Jido.Plugin.Scheduler.occurrence(context.signal)
        state = context.agent_state

        {:ok, %{state | reports: state.reports + 1, last_occurrence: occurrence.scheduled_at}}
      end
      state from: output()
    end
  end
end
```

The Scheduler adds a `scheduler` field to Agent state:

```elixir
{:ok, reminders} = Jido.start_agent(ScheduleGuide.Reminders, id: "reminders")
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(reminders)

Jido.AgentServer.agent(reminders).state.scheduler
```

The result is `%{cron: %{}}`: no recurring schedules yet.

## One-Shot Signals

`schedule/2` sends one Signal after a delay in milliseconds:

```elixir
request = ScheduleGuide.Reminders.request_signal!(%{text: "stretch", delay_ms: 200})
{:ok, committed, directives} = Jido.AgentServer.call(reminders, request)

{committed.state.reminders, committed.state.scheduler, Enum.map(directives, & &1.__struct__)}
```

The result is `{[], %{cron: %{}}, [Jido.Plugin.Scheduler.Schedule]}`. The
reminder hasn't fired, and the one-shot isn't in the `scheduler` field. It
exists only in the Scheduler's process.

```elixir
ScheduleGuide.Wait.until(fn ->
  case Jido.AgentServer.agent(reminders).state.reminders do
    [] -> :wait
    reminders -> {:ok, reminders}
  end
end)
```

The result is `{:ok, ["stretch"]}`. The delayed Signal ran its own Turn.

## Recurring Signals

`cron/4` creates or replaces a recurring schedule under a job ID.
`"* * * * * * *"` is the seven-field form with a seconds field, and fires
every second. Five-field expressions such as `"0 * * * *"` also work.

```elixir
start = ScheduleGuide.Reminders.start_reports_signal!()
{:ok, committed, _directives} = Jido.AgentServer.call(reminders, start)

committed.state.scheduler.cron["report"]
|> Map.delete(:message)
```

The result is:

```text
%{generation: 1, cron_expression: "* * * * * * *", timezone: "Etc/UTC"}
```

The entry also stores the tick Signal under `message`. Recurring schedules
are Agent state: they commit with the Turn that created them and restore with
the Agent.

Wait for two ticks:

```elixir
ScheduleGuide.Wait.until(
  fn ->
    state = Jido.AgentServer.agent(reminders).state
    if state.reports >= 2, do: {:ok, state.reports}, else: :wait
  end,
  5_000
)
```

The result is `{:ok, 2}`.

`cron/4` options:

| Option | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `timezone` | `"Etc/UTC"` | Time zone for evaluating the expression. |
| `generation` | `nil` | Adds occurrence metadata to each tick. |

## Occurrence IDs

Each tick has a new Signal ID, so the Signal ID can't tell you which
scheduled slot a tick belongs to. With `generation:` set, each tick carries
occurrence metadata, and `Scheduler.occurrence/1` reads it:

```elixir
Jido.AgentServer.agent(reminders).state.last_occurrence
```

The result is the scheduled UTC instant of the last tick, such as
`"2026-10-20T01:52:41.000000Z"`.

The occurrence also has an `id`, derived from the Jido instance, Agent ID,
partition, job ID, generation, and scheduled instant. The same slot always
produces the same ID on any node. Use it as an idempotency key, for example
to skip a report you already sent for that hour.

Increase the generation when you replace or recreate a schedule, so old and
new slots never share IDs. The occurrence ID only identifies a slot: it
doesn't save, acknowledge, or redeliver ticks.

## Cancel A Schedule

```elixir
stop = ScheduleGuide.Reminders.stop_reports_signal!()
{:ok, committed, _directives} = Jido.AgentServer.call(reminders, stop)

committed.state.scheduler
```

The result is `%{cron: %{}}`. After the commit, the Scheduler stops the job:

```elixir
before = Jido.AgentServer.agent(reminders).state.reports
Process.sleep(1_500)

{before, Jido.AgentServer.agent(reminders).state.reports}
```

Both numbers in the result are the same: no ticks arrived after the cancel.

## Test Without A Clock

The Scheduler updates its `scheduler` field in its reducer, so `cmd/3` shows
the change without starting a timer:

```elixir
agent = ScheduleGuide.Reminders.instantiate!(id: "pure")
start = ScheduleGuide.Reminders.start_reports_signal!()
{:ok, candidate, [directive]} = ScheduleGuide.Reminders.cmd(agent, start)

{Map.keys(candidate.state.scheduler.cron), directive.job_id}
```

The result is `{["report"], "report"}`. You can test what an Action schedules
with no live Server.

## Restart Behavior

| | One-shot `schedule/2` | Recurring `cron/4` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Stored in Agent state | No | Yes, in `scheduler.cron` |
| Survives a worker crash | No | Yes, from the activation checkpoint |
| Survives an Agent restart | No | Only with persistence |
| Missed slots replayed | Not applicable | No |

When the Scheduler starts, it reads `scheduler.cron` and recreates each
timer. A slot that passes while the Scheduler is down is skipped. A pending
one-shot is lost if its process stops.

If a reminder must fire, store it in your own Agent state and check for due
items from a recurring schedule, or use an external job system.

## Scheduler Options

| Option | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `retry_delay_ms` | `1_000` | Delay before retrying a recurring job that failed to start. |
| `time_scale` | `SchedEx.IdentityTimeScale` | A `SchedEx.TimeScale` module, for controlled time in tests. |

A valid cron expression with no future slot, such as one for a past year,
stays in state as a dormant job. Cancel or replace it like any other job.

## Static Schedules

`Jido.Plugin.Scheduler.DSL` adds a `schedules` section with `every:` and
`cron:` entries. In this release it validates and stores schedule data in the
definition, but it doesn't start timers. For running timers, use the
Scheduler Plugin and its Directives.

## Stop The Example

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent("reminders")
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

## Summary

- A timer firing is a Signal, and it runs a normal Turn.
- `Heartbeat` sends a fixed Signal on a fixed interval.
- `Scheduler` runs `schedule/2`, `cron/4`, and `cancel/1` Directives after
  commit.
- Recurring schedules are Agent state. One-shots aren't.
- `generation:` gives each cron slot a stable occurrence ID.

